G4521.d:
σάββατον, -ου, τό (Aram. שַׁבָּתָא, transliterated σάββατα, and this being mistaken for a pl., the sing. σάββατον was formed from it), and σάββατα, -ων, τά 
 [in LXX for שַׁבָּת, שַׁבָּתוֹן ;] 
  1. the seventh day of the week, the sabbath 
  (a) the sing. form -ον, τὸ σ.: Matt.12:8, Mark.2:27, Luke.6:5,  al.; ἡ ἡμέρα τοῦ σ. (in LXX, Exod.20:8, al., usually τῶν σ., see infr.), Luke.13:16 14:5; ὁδὸς σαββάτου, Acts.1:12 (cf. Matt.24:29); dative, of time (τῷ) σ., Luke.6:9 14:1; ἐν (τῷ) σ., Matt.12:2, Luke.6:7, John.5:16, al.; accusative, of duration, τὸ σ., Luke.23:56; κατὰ πᾶν σ., Acts.13:27 15:21 18:4; pl., σ. τρία, Acts.17:2 R, txt. (but see infr.); 
  (b) as most frequently in LXX (see Swete, Mk., 17; Thackeray, Gr., 35) the pl. form, τὰ σ. (see supr. on the Aram. form. There is also an analogy in the names of other festivals, τ. ἐγκαίνια, ἄζυμα, etc.): Matt.28:1, Col.2:16; ἡ ἡμέρα τῶν σ. (Exod.20:8, al.), Luke.4:16, Acts.13:14 16:13; dative pl. (in LXX -τοις, but 1Ma.2:38 as in NT) by metaplasmus (El., § 9, 3), σάββασι, Matt.12:1, 5 12:10-12 Mark.1:21 2:23 3:2, 4, Luke.4:31 6:2. 
  2. seven days, a week; 
  (a) the sing. form: πρώτη σαββάτσυ, Mark.16:9; δὶς τοῦ σ. (Bl., § 35, 4; 36, 13), Luke.18:12; κατὰ μίαν σαββάτου, 1 Cor.16:2; pl., σ. τρία, Acts.17:2 R, mg. (but see supr.); 
  (b) the pl. form: ἡ μία τῶν σ. (where the genitive = μετὰ τά; Soph., Lex., 43a), Matt.28:1, Mark.16:2, Luke.24:1, John.20:1, 19, Acts.20:7 
 (AS)

G4531.d:
σαλεύω
 (σάλος), [in LXX for מוֹט, זוּעַ, etc. ;] 
prop., of the action of wind, storm, etc., to agitate, shake: of a reed, Matt.11:7, Luke.7:24; a house, Luke.6:48, Acts.4:31 16:26; the earth, Heb.12:26; the heavenly bodies, Matt.24:29, Mark.13:25, Luke.21:26; of a vessel shaken in filling, Luke.6:38. Metaphorical, 
  (a) to shake, i.e. to render insecure: τὰ σαλευόμενα, τὰ μὴ., Heb.12:27; 
  (b) with accusative of person(s), to cast down from a sense of security and happiness:  Acts.2:25 (LXX); 
  (with) to unsettle or drive away: pass., before ἀπὸ τ. νοός, 2 Thess.2:2; 
  (d) to stir up: τ. ὄχλους, Acts.17:13.†
 (AS)

G4540.d:
Σαμαρία (Rec. -άρεια), -ας, ἡ (Heb. שֹׁמְרוֹן; Aram. שָׁמְרַיִן), 
 Samaria; 
  (a) the city: Acts.8:5 (and perhaps also Acts.8:9, 14); 
  (b) the region: Luke.17:11, John.4:4-5, 7, Acts.1:8 8:1 (and perhaps also Acts.8:9, 14) Acts.9:31 15:3.†
 (AS)

G4549.d:
Σαούλ, ὁ indecl. 
 (in FlJ, Σάουλος; Heb. שָׁאוּל) 
Saul; 
  (a) the King of Israel: Acts.13:21; 
  (b) the Jewish name of the Apostle Paul, used in the indecl. form only in address (cf. Σαῦλος): Acts.9:4, 17 22:7, 13 26:14.†
 (AS)

G4561.d:
σάρξ, σαρκός, ἡ 
 [in LXX chiefly for בָּשָׂר ;] 
flesh; 
  1. as in cl. generally, 
  (a) prop., of the soft substance of the animal body: 1 Cor.15:39, 2 Cor.12:7, Gal.6:13, al.; σ. καὶ αἷμα, 1 Cor.5:50; σ. καὶ ὀστέα, Luke.24:39; pl., of the flesh of many or parts of the flesh of one (cl.), Rev.17:16 19:18; φαγεῖν, Re, ll. with (cf. κατεσθίειν, 4Ki.9:36, al., and βιβρώσκειν, frequently in l.); metaphorically, Jas.5:3; mystically, φ. (τρώγειν) τὴν σ. τοῦ υἱοῦ τ. ἀνθρώπου,  John.6:52-56; 
  (b) Of the whole substance of the body, = σῶμα: Acts.2:26 (LXX), 31, 2 Cor.12:7, Gal.4:14, Eph.5:29; μία σ., Mark.10:8; εἰς σ. μίαν (Gen.2:24), Matt.19:5, Mark.10:8, 1 Cor.6:16, Eph.5:31; hence, of the material as opposite to the immaterial part of man (cf. Lft., Notes, 88): opposite to πνεῦμα, 1 Cor.5:5, 2 Cor.7:1, Col.2:5, 1 Pet.3:18, 4:6; to ψύχη, Acts.2:31, Rec.; of the present life, ἐν (τῇ) σ., Rom.7:5, Gal.2:20, Phil.1:22, 24 1 Pet.4:2; of Christ's life on earth, αἱ ἡμέραι τ. σαρκὸς αὐτοῦ, Heb.5:7; of things pertaining to the body, ἐν (τῇ) σ., Gal.6:12, 13 Phil.3:3, 4. 
  2. As in Heb. idiom, 
  (a) of a living creature: πᾶσα σ. (Heb. כָּל־בָּשָׂר; cf. Bl., §47, 9), Matt.24:22, Mark.13:20, 1 Pet.1:24; esp. of man and his mortality (Psa.55 (56):5, Sir.28:5, al.), John.1:14; πᾶσα σ. (see supr.), Luke.3:6, John.17:2, Acts.2:17, ἐν σ., 1Jo.4:2, 1 Tim.3:16; 
  (b) of natural origin and relationship (Gen.2:24, Isa.58:7, al.): τέκνα τῆς σ., Rom.9:8; κατὰ σάρκα, ib. 3, 5, 1 Cor.10:18, Gal.4:23, 29; ἡ σ. μου, Rom.11:14 (cf. Jdg.9:2, 2Ki.5:1, al.). 
  3. Of the physical nature as subject to sensation and desire (Plut.), 
  (a) without any ethical disparagement: Rom.7:18 13:14; opposite to πνεῦμα, Matt.26:41, Mark.14:38; τ. θέλημα τῆς σ., John.1:13; ἡ ἐπιθυμία τῆς σ., 1 John.2:16; pl., 2 Pet.2:18; παθεῖν σαρκί, 1 Pet.4:1; 
  (b) in ethical sense, esp. in Pauline Epp., of the flesh as the seat and vehicle of sinful desires: opposite to νοῦς, Rom.7:25; to πνεῦμα, Rom.8:4-9, 12, 13, Gal.5:16, 17 19 6:8 (cf. DB, ii, 14f.; iv, 165f.; Cremer, 844ff.). 
 (AS)

G4575.d:
σεβαστός, -ή, -όν (σεβάζομαι) 
  1. reverend, august. 
  2. In late writers, with reference to the Roman Imperial name; 
  (a) ὁ Σ., Augustus, i.e. the Roman Emperor: Acts.25:21, 25; 
  (b) Augustan: σπείρα σ., Acts.27:1 (See further, Deiss., BS, 218.) †
 (AS)

G4592.d:
σημεῖον, -ου, τό 
 [in LXX chiefly for אוֹת ;] 
a sign, mark, token; 
  (a) of that which distinguishes a person or thing from others: Matt.26:48 Luke.2:12, 2 Thess.3:17 (cf. Deiss., LAE, 153.2); before genitive epexeg., Rom.4:11; with genitive obj., Matt.24:8, 30, 2 Cor.12:12; with genitive subj., Matt.16:3; 
  (b) a sign of warning or admonition: Matt.12:39 16:4, Luke.2:34 11:29-30, 1 Cor.14:22; 
  (with) a sign portending future events (Soph., Plat. al.): Mark.13:4, Luke.21:7, 11 21:25, Acts.2:19, Rev.12:1, 3 15:1; 
  (d) of miracles and wonders (MM, xxii), regarded as signs of a divine authority: Matt.12:38-39 16:1, 4 Mark.8:11-12 Luke.11:16, 29 23:8, John.2:11, 18 2:23 4:54 6:30 10:41 12:18, Acts.4:16, 22; pl., Mark.16:17, 20, John.2:11, 23 3:2 6:2, 14 6:26 7:31 9:16 11:47 12:37 20:30, Acts.8:6, 1 Cor.1:22 (Lft., Notes, 162); the same ascribed to false teachers and demons: Matt.24:24, Mark.13:22, 2 Thess.2:9, Rev.13:13-14 16:14 19:20; σ. καὶ τέρατα (τ. καὶ σ.; cf. Tr., Syn., § xci), Matt.24:24, Mark.13:22, John.4:48, Acts.2:19, 43 4:30 5:12 6:8 7:36 14:3 15:12, Rom.15:19, 2 Thess.2:9; id. before καὶ δυνάμεις, 2 Cor.12:12, Heb.2:4; σ. καὶ δυνάμεις, Acts.8:13; δ. καὶ τ. καὶ σ., Acts.2:22; σ. διδόναι, Matt.24:24, Mark.13:22.†
 (AS)

G4606.d:
Σιδώνιος, -α, -ον
 (Σιδών), 
of Sidon, Sidonian; 
  (a) the region: sc. χώρα, Luke.4:26; 
  (b) the people: Acts.12:20.†
 (AS)

G4616.d:
σινδών, -όνος, ἡ 
 [in LXX: Jdg.14:12-13 A, Pro.31:24 (סָדִין), 1Ma.10:64 A * ;] 
fine linen cloth; 
  (a) as used for swathing dead bodies (cf. Hdt., ii, 86): Matt.27:59, Mark.15:46, Luke.23:53; 
  (b) a garment or wrap of this material (cf. ICC, Jg, l.with): Mark.14:51-52.†
 (AS)

G4622.d:
Σιών 
 indecl., 
 in NT anarth., but in LXX when used of the city of Jerusalem, ἡ Σ. 
 (Heb. צִיּוֹן), 
Zion; 
  1. the mountain: in typical sense, of the Church, Heb.12:22; of heaven, Rev.14:1. 
  2. The city, i.e. Jerusalem; in poetical sense; 
  (a) of the inhabitants: θυγάτηρ Σ, Matt.21:5, John.12:15" (LXX) ; 
  (b) in wider sense, of Israel: Rom.11:26; 
  (with) figuratively: τίθήμι ἐν Σ λίθον, Rom.9:33, 1 Pet.2:6" (LXX) .†
 (AS)

G4625.d:
σκάνδαλον, -ου, τό
 (late form of the rare word σκανδάληθρον, see LS, see word) [in LXX chiefly for מוֹקֵשׁ, מִכְשׁוֹל ;] 
prop., the bait-stick of a trap, a snare, stumbling-block (Lev.19:14, Jdth.5:1): figuratively, Rom.9:33, 1 Pet.2:8 (Isa.8:14; aliter in LXX; στερεὸν σκανδάλου in Aq.). Metaphorical, of that which causes error or sin; 
  (a) of persons: Matt.13:41 16:23; Χριστὸς ἐσταυρωμένος, 1 Cor.1:23; 
  (b) of things: Matt.18:7, Rom.11:9" (LXX) Rom.14:13, 1 John.2:10 Rev.2:14; τὸ σ. τοῦ σταυροῦ, Gal.5:11; pl., Matt.18:7, Luke.17:1, Rom.16:17.†
 SYN.: πρόσκομμα  (AS)

G4642.d:
σκληρός, -ά, -όν
 (σκέλλω, to dry), [in LXX chiefly for קָשָׁה ;] 
hard to the touch, rough, harsh, (opposite to μαλακός); metaphorically, in various uses; 
  (a) of men, hard, stern, severe: Matt.25:24; 
  (b) of things, hard, rough, violent: σκληρόν σοι (ἐστι), Acts.26:14; λόγος, John.6:60; ἄνεμος, Jas.3:4; σκληρὰ λαλεῖν, Ju 15.†
 SYN.: αὐστηρός, q.v  (AS)

G4678.d:
σοφία, -ας, ἡ 
 [in LXX chiefly for חׇכְמָה ;] 
skill, intelligence, wisdom, ranging from knowledge of the arts and matters of daily life to mental excellence in its highest and fullest sense; 
  (a) of human wisdom: 1 Cor.2:1 2:4-5, Jas.3:15, Rev.13:18 17:9; σ. Σολομῶνος, Matt.12:42, Luke.11:31; Αἰγυπτίων, Acts.7:22; Ἕλληνες σ. ζητοῦσιν, 1 Cor.1:22; σ. λόγου, 1 Cor.1:17; τ. σοφῶν, 1 Cor.1:19 (LXX); τ. κόσμου, 1 Cor.1:20-21 3:19; ἀνθρωπίνη, 1 Cor.2:13; σαρκική, 2 Cor.1:12; 
 of wisdom in spiritual things: Luke.21:15, Acts.6:3, 10 7:10, 1 Cor.2:6, Col.1:28 2:23 3:16 4:5, Jas.1:5 3:13, 17, 2 Pet.3:15; λόγος σοφίας, 1 Cor.12:8; πνεῦμα σοφίας, Eph.1:17; σ. καὶ φρόνησις, Eph.1:8; σ. καὶ σύνεσις, Col.1:9; 
  (b) of divine wisdom: of God, Rom.11:33, 1 Cor.1:21 1:24 2:7, Rev.7:12; πολυποίκιλος, Eph.3:10 
 of Christ, Matt.13:54, Mark.6:2, Luke.2:40, 52, 1 Cor.1:30 Col.2:3, Rev.5:12; 
 of wisdom personified, Matt.11:19, Luke.7:35 11:49.†
 SYN.: σύνεσις, intelligence; φρόνησις, prudence, which with σ. make up (Arist., N. Eth., i, 13) the three intellectual ἀρεταί. σ. is wisdom primary and absolute; in distinction from which φ. is practical, σύνεσις critical, both being applications of σ. in detail (cf. Lft., and ICC on Col.1:9; Lft., Notes, 317 f.; Tr., Syn., § LXXv; Cremer, 870 ff.).  (AS)

G4690.d:
σπέρμα, -τος, τό
 (σπείρω) [in LXX chiefly for זֶרַע ;] 
seed; 
  (a) of plants: Matt.13:24, 27 13:37-38, 2 Cor.9:10; pl., Matt.13:32, Mark.4:31, 1 Cor.15:33; metaphorically, of an escaping remnant (שָׂרִיד, Isa.1:9; cf. Wis.14:6; Plat., Tim., 23c; FlJ, Ant., xi, 5, 3), Rom.9:29; 
  (b) of men (as γονή; Lat. semen genitale; so in cl.; cf. in LXX, Lev.15:16, al.): Heb.11:11; metaphorically, of the divine influence, 1 John.3:9; by meton. (as frequently in poets), seed, offspring, posterity: Matt.22:24-25, Mark.12:19-22, Luke.1:55 20:28" (LXX) John.7:42 8:33, 37 Acts.3:25 7:5-6 13:23, Rom.1:3 4:13, 18 9:7-8 11:1, 2 Cor.11:22, Gal.3:16, 19, 2 Tim.2:8, Heb.2:16 11:18; pl. (FlJ, Ant., viii, 7, 6; Flat., Leg., ix, 853 with; 4Ma.18:1; in Ga, l.with, contrasted with sing., see Lft., in l; Milligan, NTD, 105 f.), Gal.3:16; of spiritual offspring, Rom.4:16, 18 9:8, Gal.3:29, Rev.12:17.†
 (AS)

G4691.d:
σπερμολόγος, -ον
 (σπέρμα, λέγω) 
a seed picker; 
  (a) prop., of birds (Arist., Aristoph, al.); 
  (b) in Attic slang, of an idler who lives on scraps picked up in the agora; hence, as subst., ὁ σ., an idle babbler: Acts.17:18.†
 (AS)

G4715.d:
στατήρ, -ῆρος, ὁ 
 [in Aq., Sm., for מִשְׁקָל ;] 
a stater; 
  (a) a weight; 
  (b) a coin (used by late writers of the Greek τετράδραχμον): Matt.17:27 26:16, WH, mg.†
 (AS)

G4733.d:
στερέωμα, -τος, τό
 (στερεόω), [in LXX: Gen.1:6 ff., Ps.19:1 150:1, Eze.1:22-26 10:1 13:5, Dan LXX, TH Dan.3:56, Dan.12:3 (רָקִיעַ), Deu.33:26 (שַׁחַק), Ps.18:2 71:3 (סֶלַע), Est.9:29, I Est.8:78 (82), Sir.43:1, 8, 1Ma.9:14 * ;] 
a solid body; 
  (a) a support, foundation (Arist., al.); metaphorically, strength (Ps.17:1-15 70:1-5, 1Mac, ll. with); steadfastness, firmness: τ. πίστεως, Col.2:5; 
  (b) the dome of heaven (believed to be a solid canopy), the firmament (LXX).†
 (AS)

G4751.d:
στόμαχος, -ου, ὁ
 (στόμα), 
prop., a mouth, an opening; 
  (a) in early Gk. writers, the throat; 
  (b) of the opening of the stomach (Arist.); 
  (with) in later writers (Plut., al.), the stomach: 1 Tim.5:23.†
 (AS)

G4820.d:
συν-βάλλω
 (Rec. συμ-), [in LXX: 2Ch.25:19 (גָּרָה hith.), Isa.46:6 (זוּל), Jer.43:3 (סוּת hi.), Wis.5:8, al. ;] 
to throw together, hence, 
  (a) of speech (before λόγους, Eur., al.), to discuss, confer (sc. λόγους): with dative of person(s), Luke.11:53, WH, mg., Acts.17:18; before πρὸς ἀλλήλους, Acts.4:15; 
  (b) to reflect, consider, ponder: ἐν τ. καρδίᾳ, Luke.2:19; 
  (with) to meet with, fall in with: Acts.20:14; in hostile sense, εἰς πόλεμον, with dative (εἰς μάχην, Polyb.), Luke.14:31; 
  (d) mid., to contribute (Polyb., al.; cf. MM, xxiv): Acts.18:27.†
 (AS)

G4864.d:
συν-αγωγή, -ῆς, ἡ 
 [in LXX chiefly for עֵדָה, also for קָהָל, etc. ;] 
prop., a bringing together; 
  1. of things, 
  (a) a gathering in of harvest; 
  (b) a collection of money. 
  2. Of persons, 
  (a) a collecting, assembling (Polyb.); 
  (b) an assembly (MM, xxiv; Deiss., LAE, 101 ff.): Rev.2:9 3:9; esp. of a Jewish religious assembly, a synagogue: Luke.12:11, Acts.9:2, al.; of a Christian assembly, Jas.2:2. By meton., of the building in which the assembly is held, a synagogue: Matt.10:17, Mark.1:21, al. (cf. Cremer, see word ἐκκλησία) 
 SYN.: ἐκκλησία (which see)  (AS)

G4921.d:
συν-ίστημι 
 [in LXX for צָוָה pi., קָהַל ni., etc.; 1Ma.12:43, 2Ma.4:24, 3Ma.1:19 (and frequently in these books) ;] 
  1. trans., 
  (a) to commend, recommend: with accusative of person(s), 2 Cor.3:1 6:4 10:12 10:18; id. with dative, Rom.16:1 (as frequently at the beginning of a letter; Deiss., LAE, 226), 2 Cor.5:12; before πρός, 2 Cor.4:2; pass., before ὑπό, 2 Cor.12:11; 
  (b) to show, prove, establish: with accusative, Rom.3:5 5:8, 2 Cor.6:4; dupl. accusative, Gal.2:13; accusative and inf., 2 Cor.7:11 
  2. Intrans., pf., συνέστηκα (as also 2 aor. and plpf.); 
  (a) to stand with or near: Luke.9:32; 
  (b) to be composed of, consist, cohere: Col.1:17 (see Lft., in l), 2 Pet.3:5.†
 (AS)

G4972.d:
σφραγίζω 
 [in LXX chiefly for חָתַם (Deu.32:34, al.), also for סָתַם (Da TH Dan.8:26) ;] 
to seal, 
  (a) for security: Matt.27:66, Rev.20:3; τ. καρπὸν τοῦτον, figuratively (cf. Deiss., BS, 238 f.; MM, xxiv; DB, iv, 427a), Rom.15:28; 
  (b) for concealment, hence, metaphorically, to hide (Da TH Dan.9:24, John.14:17):  Rev.10:4 22:10; 
  (with) for distinction, Rev.7:3-8; metaphorically, Eph.4:30; mid., 2 Cor.1:22; 
  (d) for authentication (Est.8:8): John.3:33 6:27 (cf. κατα-σφραγίζω).†
 (AS)

G4988.d:
Σωσθένης, -ου, ὁ 
 Sosthenes; 
  (a) a Jewish ruler: Acts.18:7; 
  (b) a Christian: 1 Cor.1:1.†
 (AS)
